<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Embedded devices from wildlife monitoring stations to clinical wearables require local AI inference due to latency, communication, or privacy constraints. Optimizing models for heterogeneous microcontrollers (MCUs) requires simultaneously satisfying hard physical constraints on memory, power, and temperature while preserving accuracy, a multidimensional optimization that is today performed manually by experts. We ask whether an LLM agent can autonomously navigate this complex, multi-turn pipeline guided by real hardware feedback, and introduce a hardware-in-the-loop agent arena in which the agent iteratively refines both model and firmware---compiling, flashing, and measuring on real hardware---to enable closed-loop optimization. Frontier models, including Claude Opus 4.7 and Gemini 3.1 Pro, fail entirely without hardware feedback (0% deployment success), whereas our hardware-in-the-loop formulation achieves the first successful deployment within three iterations and can surpass human expert results within seven. This agentic co-optimization achieves 250× compression for vision models with &lt;3.3% accuracy loss and 400× for audio with &lt;6% Feature Error Rate loss, enabling battery-free operation on a commercial MCU via solar harvesting. We demonstrate practical impact in two real-world systems: an elk-detection camera trap (96.7% accuracy) and a phonetic-transcription wearable (8.44% FER) for child development research.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
        
<span id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_inline-block fas fa-github" aria-hidden="true">
</span> <a href="https://github.com/ubicomplab/embedded-arena" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/ubicomplab/embedded-arena</a></span></span></span></p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Advances in large, cloud-based AI models have unlocked remarkable capabilities across vision, language, and multimodal reasoning, but leave a gap for applications with stringent latency, privacy, or connectivity requirements. Remote wildlife cameras must detect and classify species of interest without reliable network access <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Desai et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al. (2026)</a></cite>, mobile devices may process audio and biosignals locally to avoid the privacy risks and power costs of cloud streaming <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahmed et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chatterjee et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Larson et al. (2011)</a></cite>, and physical AI systems with safety-critical perception-action cycles cannot tolerate round-trip latency to the cloud. Such applications demand small models that run directly on microcontroller (MCU) class hardware. These devices have many orders of magnitude lower clock speeds, memory, and storage than datacenter GPUs, requiring substantial model compression and optimization to utilize the growing hardware acceleration capabilities being built into MCUs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Curtiss et al. (2022)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Deploying AI in this heterogeneous, rapidly evolving hardware landscape requires multidimensional, system-level optimization that satisfies hard physical constraints (e.g., memory footprint, power consumption, thermal limits) while maximizing model accuracy.
On the hardware side, this landscape is a moving target: the STM32F7 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2020)</a></cite> MCU series alone ships in 22 silicon variants, each with distinct memory configurations and AI accelerators.
On the model side, practitioners must navigate compression techniques (e.g., quantization, operator substitution), each of which interacts differently with the target chip’s processing capabilities.
These optimization choices are coupled and competing. For example, for object detection on MCUs, reducing camera resolution saves activation memory and power draw but directly degrades accuracy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bompani et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
Today navigating these trade-offs is performed manually by experts who hold rare, joint expertise in embedded systems and machine learning (ML). Skilled practitioners using deployment frameworks such as LiteRT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google ()</a></cite> spend weeks optimizing a single model on real hardware for a single deployment—a process that must be repeated from scratch for every new chip <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2020)</a></cite>.
In this paper, we ask: <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">can we design LLM agents that autonomously navigate the edge AI deployment pipeline, guided by measurements from real hardware?</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To answer this question, we construct a <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">hardware-in-the-loop (HIL)</span> environment in which the agent iteratively produces, compiles, flashes, and refines firmware subject to physical feedback. We observe that even state-of-the-art models like Claude Opus 4.7 fail entirely in this setting when restricted to software-only feedback.
Closing the loop with physical measurements, such as power and temperature directly from the device, guides the agent toward firmware that actually works on the device. HIL raises deployment success rates from 0% to 80% on the hardest tasks.
We formalize this loop as a continuous optimization problem by reducing physical measurements to a programmatically scorable scalar objective, enabling iterative refinement. This transforms an opaque, expert-gated process into an accessible workflow, letting practitioners without joint hardware-AI expertise deploy state-of-the-art models on resource-constrained devices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our HIL formulation also addresses a gap in existing agentic systems and LLM benchmarks.
While LLM-based agents have demonstrated impressive autonomy in complex workflows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2026)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2026)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karpathy ()</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nam et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aygün et al. (2026)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gottweis et al. (2026)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chi et al. (2026)</a></cite>, benchmarks for evaluating them on long-horizon, system-level optimization remain rare.
Most (e.g., SWE-bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jimenez et al. (2023)</a></cite>, TerminalBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill et al. (2025)</a></cite>, HumanEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2021)</a></cite>) focus entirely on the digital domain, evaluating agents on isolated, human-curated single-turn tasks rather than end-to-end system optimization.
Because these tasks are scored on binary correctness, they lack a mechanism for continuous self-improvement and saturate quickly as models advance, requiring periodic manual updates.
A smaller family of Kaggle-style competitions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aygün et al. (2026)</a></cite> poses open-ended optimization scored by continuous software metrics (e.g., held-out accuracy) to support iterative hill-climbing.
Our HIL arena inherits this open-ended optimization paradigm but extends it to performance metrics measured directly from the physical world at test time, where results vary with ambient conditions and component state, forcing agents to reason about real hardware behavior.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.16190v1/figures/teaser.jpg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/143;" width="476" height="143" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">AI agents iteratively optimize models to run on MCUs within their tight power, thermal, and compute constraints using hardware-in-the-loop feedback. We demonstrate successful model optimization for real-world applications, including wildlife detection and child speech monitoring.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize our contributions as follows:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We are the first to formalize the deployment of AI on resource-constrained devices as a hardware-in-the-loop agent arena for iterative optimization, and physically build the end-to-end evaluation infrastructure.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We perform a comprehensive study of how context and hardware feedback shape the artifacts produced by LLM agents.
HIL achieves the first successful deployment within three iterations and <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">can surpass human expert results within seven</span>, where frontier LLMs fail entirely without it.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We achieve 250× compression for vision models with &lt;3.3% accuracy loss, and 400× for audio with &lt;6% Feature Error Rate (FER), also reducing power draw by over 58% and peak device temperature rise by over 40%.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate practical impact in two real-world deployments developed in direct collaboration with domain practitioners: a solar-powered camera trap for elk detection achieving 96.7% accuracy under 7 mW average power, and a children’s head-worn device with 8.44% on-device FER for phonetic transcription.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Hardware-in-the-Loop Arena</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present a hardware-in-the-loop arena in which an LLM agent must generate, compile, flash, and iteratively refine software subject to <em id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">physical</em> feedback signals returned by real hardware.
Unlike software-only benchmarks, our arena closes the loop with the physical world: rewards are not computed by scoring functions, but are <em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">measured</em> by instruments attached to the device under test.
We now formalize this as a constrained optimization problem over real hardware, then instantiate it across three physically distinct axes: model compression, power minimization, and thermal management.
The formalism we develop in §<a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Problem Formalization ‣ 2 Hardware-in-the-Loop Arena ‣ Embedded Arena: Iterative Optimization via Hardware Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> is general to any constrained hardware target.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:30%;"><img src="2606.16190v1/figures/hardware_setup.jpg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:143/143;" width="143" height="143" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Hardware setup.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Problem Formalization</h3>

<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hardware constraint vector.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a target hardware platform characterized by a <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">constraint vector</em></p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}=(h_{1},h_{2},\ldots,h_{K})\in\mathbb{R}^{K}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}=(h_{1},h_{2},\ldots,h_{K})\in\mathbb{R}^{K},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where each component <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> encodes a physical resource limit (e.g., flash storage in bytes, static RAM in bytes, peak supply current in amperes, maximum junction temperature in kelvin, supported operators).
A solution is <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">deployable</em> only if all <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> constraints are simultaneously satisfied.
We note that <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math> can be automatically identified from datasheets via web search and verified using a combination of the compiler toolchain.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Solution space.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A central design choice of our arena is that the solution space <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is entirely open-ended. The agent produces a software artifact <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, which may be firmware source code, a serialized neural network, a linker configuration, or any combination thereof.
The arena is <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">agent-agnostic</em> and imposes no constraints on how <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed: the agent may employ any architecture or strategy (e.g., retrieval-augmented generation, multi-agent architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2026)</a></cite>) and is judged solely on deployability and physical performance on the target hardware.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, the build-and-flash operator <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:\mathcal{S}\times\mathbb{R}^{K}\to\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:\mathcal{S}\times\mathbb{R}^{K}\to\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> returns <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> compiles, boots successfully, and executes the required workload correctly on the device, and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.
When <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(s,\mathbf{h})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(s,\mathbf{h})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, a physical measurement <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}:\mathcal{S}\times\mathbb{R}^{K}\to\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}:\mathcal{S}\times\mathbb{R}^{K}\to\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> returns a vector of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> empirically observed quantities <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}=\mathcal{M}(s,\mathbf{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}=\mathcal{M}(s,\mathbf{h})</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., inference accuracy, peak current draw, peak core temperature).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Optimization objective.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each benchmark task is defined by a tuple <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=(\mathbf{h},\,f,\,\mathbf{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=(\mathbf{h},\,f,\,\mathbf{c})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{M}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{M}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a task-specific objective to optimize, and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes task-level feasibility constraints that complement the hard physical limits in <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math> and must be satisfied for <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> to be valid (e.g., a minimum accuracy floor).
The agent’s goal is to find a deployable artifact <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> that minimizes the task objective:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\star}=\operatorname*{arg\,min}_{s\in\mathcal{S}}\;f\!\left(\mathcal{M}(s,\mathbf{h})\right)\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\Phi(s,\mathbf{h})=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mpadded style="width:0.427em;" width="0.427em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\star}=\operatorname*{arg\,min}_{s\in\mathcal{S}}\;f\!\left(\mathcal{M}(s,\mathbf{h})\right)\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\Phi(s,\mathbf{h})=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Because each exploration round consumes real resources (e.g., LLM inference, compilation, physical hardware measurement), the number of iterations <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> before reaching a solution is penalized as a separate metric.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Iterative agent loop.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The benchmark is structured as a multi-turn interaction
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{s_{0}\}\cup\{(s_{t},\,o_{t})\}_{t=1}^{N_{\max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{s_{0}\}\cup\{(s_{t},\,o_{t})\}_{t=1}^{N_{\max}}</annotation></semantics></math> (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Embedded Arena: Iterative Optimization via Hardware Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
At <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the agent is provided an initial artifact <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., a <span id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">model.py</span> specifying a pre-trained model architecture, or a <span id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">firmware.c</span> implementing a reference inference pipeline) together with the hardware constraint vector <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math> and the task objective <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.
The initial artifact <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as a starting point for optimization and need not satisfy the hardware constraints outright (e.g., <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(s_{0},\mathbf{h})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(s_{0},\mathbf{h})=0</annotation></semantics></math> when a pre-trained model exceeds the target flash budget).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At each subsequent step <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the agent submits a modified artifact <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>; the benchmark executes <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(s_{t},\mathbf{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(s_{t},\mathbf{h})</annotation></semantics></math> and, if <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi=1</annotation></semantics></math>, runs <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, then returns the full observation <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{t}=(\Phi(s_{t},\mathbf{h}),\,\mathbf{m}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{t}=(\Phi(s_{t},\mathbf{h}),\,\mathbf{m}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> to the agent as a structured natural-language summary (e.g., current draw, measured chip temperature).
If <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(s_{t},\mathbf{h})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(s_{t},\mathbf{h})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the agent receives only the failure diagnostic without physical measurements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The agent is allotted a budget of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> attempts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Agent memory and context.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At each round <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the agent’s artifact <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is written to a dedicated file (e.g., <span id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">iteration_t.c</span>), preserving the full artifact history on disk.
The observation <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is appended as a structured natural-language summary directly to the agent’s context window (see Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Structured Natural Language Feedback Example ‣ Embedded Arena: Iterative Optimization via Hardware Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>).
The agent is additionally equipped with tools to read and edit local files.
The agent may query all prior artifacts <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{s_{0},\ldots,s_{t-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{s_{0},\ldots,s_{t-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> on demand to inform <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We preserve the full failure trajectory rather than maintaining only the current best artifact via version-control rollback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Andrej (2026)</a></cite>, so the agent has the <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">option</em> to understand past failures when proposing <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Benchmark score.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To enable fair comparison across agents, we define the benchmark score <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> for a task as</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}=f\!\left(\mathcal{M}(s^{\star},\mathbf{h})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.427em;" width="0.427em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}=f\!\left(\mathcal{M}(s^{\star},\mathbf{h})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is the best deployable solution found by the agent within <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> rounds, and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is defined so that lower <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> is always better (maximization objectives are negated).
If no deployable solution satisfying <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is found within <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> rounds, the agent receives <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When two agents achieve equal <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, we break ties by the total number of attempts <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq N_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq N_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>, including attempts where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(s_{t},\mathbf{h})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(s_{t},\mathbf{h})=0</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., the artifact fails to compile or boot). Lower <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is better.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Task Instantiations on MCUs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MCUs represent a particularly demanding instantiation of the framework in §<a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Problem Formalization ‣ 2 Hardware-in-the-Loop Arena ‣ Embedded Arena: Iterative Optimization via Hardware Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>: with typically 128 KB–512 KB of SRAM, 512 KB–4 MB of flash, computing throughput two to three orders of magnitude below the tens of TOPS available on a smartphone SoC, and power budgets measured in milliwatts, they stress every dimension of the hardware constraint vector <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math> simultaneously.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We instantiate three tasks along distinct axes of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math>: model, power, and thermal (see Appendix for full objective derivations and evaluation variants).
All three physical dimensions can, in principle, be incorporated jointly into a single <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>; we decompose them into separate tasks to enable clean, interpretable evaluation of each.
The tasks are defined solely in terms of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{h},f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{h},f)</annotation></semantics></math> and the feedback <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and therefore can be extended to any hardware target for which <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> can be automated; our experiments span multiple MCU families (§<a href="#S3" title="3 Experiments ‣ Embedded Arena: Iterative Optimization via Hardware Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.1 </span>Task 1: Model Compression</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Deploying a neural network on an MCU requires satisfying all <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math>: flash (model weights), SRAM (activations), and MCU-specific constraints such as supported operators and quantization formats.
Given a pre-trained model and calibration dataset <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and evaluation dataset <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, the agent produces a compressed artifact <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> derived from <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., via quantization or distillation) that maximizes on-device inference accuracy <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\text{acc}}=\mathcal{M}_{\text{acc}}(s,\mathcal{D})\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mtext>acc</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>acc</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\text{acc}}=\mathcal{M}_{\text{acc}}(s,\mathcal{D})\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> subject to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(s,\mathbf{h})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(s,\mathbf{h})=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
When two agents achieve equal <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}=f_{\text{comp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>comp</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}=f_{\text{comp}}</annotation></semantics></math>, ties are broken by the number of attempts <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, rewarding sample-efficient optimization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.2 </span>Task 2: Power Minimization</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Excessive <em id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">peak current</em> <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{peak}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>peak</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{peak}}</annotation></semantics></math> can trigger brownouts due to limits on battery discharge rate or permanent hardware damage even when the time-averaged current is well within budget.
For battery-operated devices, <em id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">total energy per event</em> <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> determines operational lifetime on a fixed battery capacity.
The agent minimizes one or both quantities—instantiated as separate variants targeting <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{peak}}(\mathbf{m})=I_{\text{peak}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>peak</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>peak</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{peak}}(\mathbf{m})=I_{\text{peak}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{energy}}(\mathbf{m})=E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>energy</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{energy}}(\mathbf{m})=E</annotation></semantics></math>—subject to a minimum accuracy floor <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\text{acc}}\geq m_{\text{acc}}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mtext>acc</mtext></msub><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mtext>acc</mtext><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\text{acc}}\geq m_{\text{acc}}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Power Minimization ‣ Embedded Arena: Iterative Optimization via Hardware Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.3 </span>Task 3: Thermal Management</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Device temperature governs hardware reliability and, critically for wearable applications, user safety: skin-contact temperatures above approximately 317 K (44 °C) can cause low-temperature burns with prolonged contact <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moritz and Henriques (1947)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al. (2023)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{amb}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>amb</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{amb}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the measured ambient temperature and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{dev}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>dev</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{dev}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> (K) the device surface temperature over the elapsed time <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,\tau]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,\tau]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The agent minimizes peak temperature rise <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta T=T_{\text{peak}}-T_{\text{amb}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>peak</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>amb</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta T=T_{\text{peak}}-T_{\text{amb}}</annotation></semantics></math> under a controlled ambient load, with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> as a hard safety ceiling in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Because <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta T</annotation></semantics></math> depends on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{amb}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>amb</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{amb}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we evaluate each device across multiple controlled ambient conditions, from room temperature to a surface approximating skin contact; details are in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Thermal Management ‣ Embedded Arena: Iterative Optimization via Hardware Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experiments</h2>

<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.16190v1/figures/compression_high.jpg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:524/116;" width="524" height="116" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">HIL Enables Iterative Improvement in Model Compression.<span id="S3.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (a) YOLO on MAX78000, where Gemini 3.1 Pro surpasses the human expert best model <math id="S3.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14.15\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>14.15</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14.15\%</annotation></semantics></math> by iteration 7.
(b) Wav2Vec2 on STM32N6. In both tasks agents hill-climb steadily once a valid <math id="S3.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is established.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To disentangle the contribution of physical feedback from LLMs’ parametric knowledge of embedded development, we evaluate three MCU targets (ESP32-S3, MAX78000, STM32N6) under three information settings:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Score (Sco)</span>: a baseline where the agent receives the task description and initial artifact <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, with access to a sandboxed environment supporting file manipulation and code execution. At each turn it observes only the binary feasibility check <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(s_{t},\mathbf{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(s_{t},\mathbf{h})</annotation></semantics></math> and the scalar optimization objective value <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{t}=f(\mathcal{M}(s_{t},\mathbf{h}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{t}=f(\mathcal{M}(s_{t},\mathbf{h}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Documentation (Doc)</span>: the agent is additionally provided expert-curated device datasheets and toolchain documentation describing target constraints (e.g., supported operators, memory limits, required configuration formats) as part of <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and, except for model compression, may use web search to look up further details. This baseline is motivated by our finding that without such materials, frontier LLMs fail even basic MCU deployment questions (Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Benchmarking LLM Knowledge of MCU Deployment ‣ Embedded Arena: Iterative Optimization via Hardware Feedback" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our <span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardware-in-the-loop (HIL)</span>: extends Doc with physical feedback <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> after each iteration, including resource utilization, serial logs, and deployment statistics.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate six frontier LLMs: GPT-5.4, GPT-5.4-mini, Claude Opus 4.7, Claude Sonnet 4.6, Gemini 3.1 Pro, and Gemini 3 Flash, each at both <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">low</span> and <span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">high</span> reasoning effort. Each run is capped at <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\max}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\max}=10</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, with up to 30 tool calls per iteration.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Model Compression</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Model compression evaluates whether agents can reduce a model’s footprint to meet MCU hardware constraints while preserving task performance.
We instantiate this task across vision and audio, two common modalities in edge, to demonstrate generalizability, on two platforms with diverse constraints: MAX78000 (max 442 KB of model weights; very limited operator support) and STM32N6 (4.2 MB of live model weights; more flexible operator support).
After the agent produces a compressed artifact <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the binary check <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(s_{t},\mathbf{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(s_{t},\mathbf{h})</annotation></semantics></math> confirms (i) the compressed model is trainable (i.e., we can perform backpropagation on the weights and improve the loss during training) and, (ii) can be compiled and run on the respective hardware with the code and network sidecars the agent provides. The score <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is test-set performance of the trained compressed model, i.e., <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{r}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{r}=0</annotation></semantics></math> on the target MCU.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table ltx_align_floatright" style="width:53%;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span><span id="S3.T1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Best On-Device Accuracy on MAX78000.</span> Values are mAP50-95 (YOLO11, COCO); <em id="S3.T1.12" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#1967D2;">higher</em> is better. <span id="S3.T1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#A50E0E;">✗</span> indicate no successful deployment within <math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> attempts. Three HIL agents surpass the human expert baseline (14.15%).</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.14" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.14.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.14.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.14.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Model</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.14.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.14.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Sco</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.14.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.14.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Doc</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.14.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.14.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">HIL</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.14.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.14.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S3.T1.14.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">Thinking</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.14.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.14.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">L</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.14.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.14.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">H</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.14.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.14.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">L</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.14.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.14.2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">H</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.14.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.14.2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">L</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.14.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.14.2.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">H</span></th></tr>
</thead>
